ense and  under Rahm Emmanuel's administration a  few years back the city really took that  to a new level and they suspended more  driver's licenses and they had in years  prior and Melissa Sanchez discovered  tens of thousands of Chicagoans  actually had to file for bankruptcy as a  way to relieve the debt and get their  drivers licenses back I talked to a  woman who had a hundred thousand dollars  in ticket debt and and they couldn't pay  for it but they really needed their  licenses to drive and they couldn't  afford to get him a city payment plants  to be able to start that process to  reinstate their licenses and the only  legal option that they could afford was  filing for chapter 13 bankruptcy forced  into bankruptcy over parking tickets  exactly and then working with Chicago  radio station WBEZ ProPublica tracked  tens of thousands of the tickets and  discovered they were being written  mostly in primarily african-american  neighborhoods and that let us say more  definitively police are issuing more  tickets a disproportionate share of  tickets in the south and west sides of  Chicago where there's more low-income  black families living whenever you wake  up in the morning you know there's a  possibility of getting your car towed  you feel like a criminal you feel like a  piece of yourself is gonna be gone  you know because yes it's just a car but  it's my car but it took a year and a  half long series of stories to get City  Hall and then Mayor Rahm Emanuel to  acknowledge the problem she says it  wasn't until a few weeks before he  ultimately left office that he publicly  acknowledged yes there might be a  problem and we will work to fix it but  now the reporting is having a major  impact Chicago's new mayor Lori  Lightfoot says she was outraged by the  findings this is way more than just  about ticketed this is about restoring  people's dignity this is about being  able to provide for your family this is  about giving people a real chance to get  to work 